=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk report that PC3 (10.3.0.100) in Office B is "completely isolated" does not correspond to any real fault in the network. Direct probing from PC3 confirms zero packet loss to every reachable destination: its default gateway (10.3.0.1 on dswb1), all intra-Office-B hosts (srv1 at 10.5.0.4), all cross-office hosts in Office A (pc1 at 10.1.0.100, pc2 at 10.1.0.101, laptop1 at 10.6.0.100), and ISPA's simulated internet loopbacks (172.253.62.100, 152.250.31.93). The control plane is equally healthy: every OSPF adjacency across the full hierarchy (r1 ↔ csw1/csw2, csw1/csw2 ↔ dswa1/dswa2/dswb1/dswb2, dswb1 ↔ dswb2) is in Full state, all subnets are in every router's RIB, and r1's NAT MASQUERADE rules for 10.3.0.0/24 are present and operational. The STP topology on aswb2 is normal: eth1 (→dswb1) is forwarding, eth2 (→dswb2) is STP-blocked as the redundant uplink — exactly the expected design. The two destinations that failed in initial probing (8.8.8.8 and 10.2.0.100) reflect the lab environment itself: 8.8.8.8 is not a simulated host, and 10.2.0.0/24 has no host assigned at .100. The root cause of the ticket is a false user report — no network fault exists. No corrective action is required; the on-call engineer should close the ticket as a false alarm after confirming with the end user.
=== END ===